    {
      "SurveyID": "SV_1X34YGdTiaOe2G2",
      "Element": "SQ",
      "PrimaryAttribute": "QID34",
      "SecondaryAttribute": "Q234cf8e0cacf42d9-chunk_21b13a7046fc06d1f09325d9",
      "TertiaryAttribute": null,
      "Payload": {
        "QuestionText": "<p><strong>EDOS Label</strong></p><p><strong>1.1 threats of harm</strong> – Expressing intent, willingness or desire to harm an individual woman or group of women. This could include, but is not limited to: physical, sexual, emotional or privacy-based forms of harm.</p><br><p><strong>Paragraph</strong></p><div style=\"white-space: pre-wrap\">Digital Threats: The Landscape of TFGBV > Targeted Violence against Influential Women<br><br>In a survey of women parliamentarians from 39 countries conducted by the Inter-Parliamentary Union, 81 percent reported that they experienced violence on digital platforms.<sup>52</sup>\n\nAmong women parliamentarians in the European Union, 59 percent had been the target of sexist attacks on online social networks.<sup>53</sup>\n\nIn member states of the African Union, 42 percent of women politicians have received online death threats, rape threats, or threats of beatings and abductions.<sup>54</sup>\n\nIn Southeast Asia, 50 percent of women politicians have faced online abuse.<sup>55</sup>\n\nFor women who are not part of the ethnic majority in a given country, the abuse intensifies. In the United Kingdom, a study found that nearly half of all abuse against female members of Parliament (MPs) on X (formerly Twitter) targeted Diane Abbot, the first black woman elected to the British Parliament, and even when she was removed from the sample, women of color received 30 percent more abuse than white women.<sup>56</sup></div><br>",
        "DataExportTag": "Q234cf8e0cacf42d9-chunk_21b13a7046fc06d1f09325d9",
        "QuestionType": "MC",
        "Selector": "SAVR",
        "SubSelector": "TX",
        "DataVisibility": {
          "Private": false,
          "Hidden": false
        },
        "Configuration": {
