    {
      "SurveyID": "SV_1X34YGdTiaOe2G2",
      "Element": "SQ",
      "PrimaryAttribute": "QID64",
      "SecondaryAttribute": "Qf773d831ae5c9f34-chunk_6eac20fdad8679e34c35593e",
      "TertiaryAttribute": null,
      "Payload": {
        "QuestionText": "<p><strong>EDOS Label</strong></p><p><strong>3.2 immutable gender differences and gender stereotypes</strong> – Asserting immutable, natural or otherwise essential differences between men and women. In some cases, this could be in the form of using women’s traits to attack men. Most sexist jokes will fall into this category.</p><br><p><strong>Paragraph</strong></p><div style=\"white-space: pre-wrap\">Where does sexism come from?<br><br>We all create stereotypes, which manifest themselves as unconscious bias. In fact, studies have shown that people who believe they are objective, or that they are not sexist, are less objective and more likely to behave in a sexist way (<sup>19</sup>).\n\nGender stereotypes were first catalogued in the United States in the 1970s. They were based on the characteristics reported as being the most socially desirable in women and men. Stereotypically feminine attributes included gullibility, shyness and compassion, while stereotypically masculine attributes included aggressiveness, independence and leadership ability.\n\n<hr/>\n\n(<sup>17</sup>) Rippon, 2019.\n(<sup>18</sup>) Heilman, M. E., Manzi, F. and Braun, S., ‘Presumed incompetent: perceived lack of fit and gender bias in recruitment and selection’, Handbook of gendered careers in management: getting in, getting on, getting out, Edward Elgar, Cheltenham, 2015, pp. 90–104 (http://dro.dur.ac.uk/16470/).\n(<sup>19</sup>) Criado Perez, C., Invisible Women – Exposing data bias in a world designed for men, Chatto & Windus, London, 2019.\nPart 1.Understand</div><br>",
        "DataExportTag": "Qf773d831ae5c9f34-chunk_6eac20fdad8679e34c35593e",
        "QuestionType": "MC",
        "Selector": "SAVR",
        "SubSelector": "TX",
